{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## MEASURED EVIDENCE CONTRADICTS THIS TICKET'S PREMISE — and it came from this ticket's own instrument\n\nThis ticket asserts, in its title, that the completion verifier \"exhausts its turn budget on\ngenuine breadth, NOT on a loop\".\n\nNew measurement, 2026-08-05, from the completion-verification run on ticket\n94a3-1323-3497-4f59:\n\n    max_consecutive_repeat = 131\n    on ONE identical `search_files` signature, out of 260 total tool calls\n    failed at the DEFAULT step budget, then failed IDENTICALLY at REBAR_LLM_MAX_STEPS=1200\n\n131 consecutive repeats of the same call signature is a non-progressing loop by any reasonable\ndefinition. Raising the budget \ndid not change the outcome, which is the discriminating test between\n\"needs more room\" and \"is not progressing\" — breadth would have completed with 1200 steps.\n\n## The irony worth recording\n\nThe telemetry that produced this number is THIS TICKET'S OWN WORK. a89d's single landed change\n(commit 5f00bf5253, \"report tool-call repetition on exhaustion\") satisfied AC5 by adding exactly\nthis repetition reporting. The instrument built to characterise the failure has now measured a\nfailure mode the ticket's title rules out.\n\nThat is a good outcome for the instrument and a problem for the premise.\n\n## What this means — two readings, not yet distinguished\n\n1. **The premise is simply wrong** and the exhaustion always was (or has become) a loop. If so\n   this ticket's ACs 1-4 are aimed at the wrong target: \"make evidence-gathering directed\" and\n   \"stop treating output_token_limit as a hard ceiling\" do not fix a 131x repeat.\n2. **There are TWO failure modes** that both surface as budget exhaustion — genuine breadth (the\n   9fd4 / db1c cases this ticket cites) and a non-progressing loop (the 94a3 case). If so, both\n   are real and this ticket should be scoped to the first, with the loop tracked separately.\n\nI did NOT determine which. Reading 2 is plausible because the cited cases predate today's\nmeasurement, and nobody has re-run 9fd4 or db1c with the repetition telemetry in place — which\nis now the obvious next step and is cheap.\n\n## Related\n\nBug 46a2-9c1a-d855-4706 was filed today for the loop, from the 94a3 run, with a loop-guard design.\nIf reading 1 holds, 46a2 and this ticket are the same bug and one should be closed as duplicate.\nIf reading 2 holds, they are siblings. Deciding that is the first thing to do here.\n\nUNVERIFIED LEAD, flagged rather than asserted: the looping call is `search_files`, and commit\n85805e02f1 (\"bf31: search_files must not fake '(no matches)'\") changed that tool's behaviour\ntoday. Whether a more honest empty result changes retry behaviour is speculation — I have not\ntested it, and it should be checked before being believed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785954502545754001,"uuid":"9c06a0aa-94da-4537-bcd5-e7f6c4241cc7"}